(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Procedural Execution of Brainwriting 6-3-5:) Tj
T*
(   - 6 participants sit in a circle. Each receives a worksheet containing a 6-row by 3-column) Tj
T*
(   grid.) Tj
T*
(   - Round 1 \(5 mins\): Each participant writes 3 distinct, concise ideas in Row 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Rotation: Sheets are passed to the person on the right.) Tj
T*
(   - Round 2 \(5 mins\): Participants read the 3 ideas written above them, using them as cognitive) Tj
T*
(   springboards to develop, adapt, or build 3 new ideas in Row 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Process repeats for 6 rounds \(30 minutes total\), generating 108 documented ideas.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Elimination of Production Blocking:) Tj
T*
(   - In verbal meetings, cognitive throughput is strictly serialized; while one person speaks for) Tj
T*
(   3 minutes, 5 others wait, causing their working memory chunks to fade.) Tj
T*
(   - Brainwriting parallelizes generation: 100% of participants write simultaneously, increasing) Tj
T*
(   informational throughput by 600%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Elimination of Evaluation Apprehension:) Tj
T*
(   - Verbal brainstorming requires social courage to interrupt peers and speak into a quiet room.) Tj
T*
(   Junior engineers worry about executive judgment.) Tj
T*
(   - Brainwriting is conducted in absolute silence. Ideas are written anonymously on evolving) Tj
T*
(   sheets where individual ownership blurs as peers piggyback, democratizing creative agency.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
