(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the 'Buy-a-Feature' collaborative game. How does pricing features based on actual engineering story points force enterprise customers to reveal their true willingness to pay?) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Mechanics of the Buy-a-Feature Game \(Luke Hohmann\):) Tj
T*
(   - Traditional customer feature requests are boundless; when asked what they want on surveys,) Tj
T*
(   customers say 'Everything!'.) Tj
T*
(   - In Buy-a-Feature, 6-8 representative customers gather around a table. A menu of 20) Tj
T*
(   prospective features is presented, each priced directly proportional to the actual engineering) Tj
T*
(   effort and cost required to build it \(e.g., Simple Export = $50; Real-Time Sync = $800\).) Tj
T*
(   - Each customer is given a fixed budget of play money \(e.g., $300\), which is deliberately) Tj
T*
(   insufficient to buy the expensive features alone.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Forcing Collaboration and Negotiation:) Tj
T*
(   - To purchase high-value, expensive infrastructure features \($800\), customers are forced to) Tj
T*
(   pool their money together, triggering intense, authentic negotiation:) Tj
T*
(   * 'If you put $200 toward the API Gateway, I will put in $300, and we both get what we need.') Tj
T*
(   * Customers fiercely debate which features deliver genuine operational ROI and which are) Tj
T*
(   superficial toys.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Quantitative Insights for Product Engineering:) Tj
T*
(   - Uncovers True Willingness to Pay: Distinguishes loud, superficial requests from deep) Tj
T*
(   commercial needs.) Tj
T*
(   - Accurately calibrates engineering roadmap resource allocation against verified customer) Tj
T*
(   willingness to fund development.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
